<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harald Fairhair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 850 – c. 932</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Harald Finehair, Harald I of Norway · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Ynglings</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harald Fairhair is remembered as the first king of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. According to the sagas he subdued the petty kingdoms of the west coast and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Battle of Hafrsfjord</a>, traditionally dated around 872. Later tradition held that his rule drove chieftains to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>, although much of his story was written down centuries later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair.jpg" alt="Harald Fairhair" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jón Þórðarson and Magnús Þórhallsson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flateyjarbok_Haraldr_Halfdan_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost nothing of Harald's biography is certain. A few fragments of praise poems by his court poet Þorbjörn Hornklofi survive, but the accounts of his life come from sagas written down about three centuries after his lifetime, none of them older than the twelfth century. The sagas differ on many points. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, written around 1230, is the most elaborate but not the oldest or most reliable.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> says he succeeded his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black</a>, who drowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/randsfjorden">Randsfjorden</a>, to several small kingdoms in Vestfold, with his mother's brother Duke Guthorm as protector. He is described as a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Yngling dynasty</a>. The sagas also differ on his mother's ancestry and on his age at accession, which is given as ten in some accounts and sixteen in the Flateyjarbók.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to Fagrskinna and Egil's Saga, Harald vowed not to cut his hair until he had become overlord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. He is said to have fought many battles, the decisive one at Hafrsfjord against Kjotve the Rich and Haklang, after which all of Norway paid him tribute. Ragnvald <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> then cut his hair and gave him the name Fairhair, where before he had been called lúfa, apparently meaning matted or shock hair. Old Norse fagr means beautiful rather than blond.</p><p>Egil's Saga says the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> were then heavily taxed. Anyone suspected of rebellion could flee, become a tenant, or lose hands and feet, and the saga says most chose to flee. Íslendingabók, the earliest narrative source, differs from later tradition in not presenting Harald as the cause of settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>. Historia Norwegiæ, about 1220, says he was the first to rule the whole coastal region rather than all of Norway.</p><p>In old age, the sagas say, he shared power with his favourite son Eirik Bloodaxe. The sagas give him from 11 to 20 sons, twelve of them named as kings, and their quarrels continued into the next reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Since the nineteenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was in a personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, Harald has been a national icon and a symbol of independence, and in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries he was regarded as having unified Norway into one kingdom. His standing as a historical figure has been questioned. Peter Sawyer began to cast doubt on the sagas in 1976, and around 2000 a few historians argued that Harald did not exist, or not in the way the sagas describe him.</p><p>Even the voyage to the west that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a> describe, to clear the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> refugees, is no longer accepted without question; Thomson wrote in 2008 that it comes as a shock to realise it might not be true.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harald Fairhair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founding is traditionally credited to <b>Harald Fairhair</b>, who according to the sagas defeated a coalition of rival kings at the Battle of Hafrsfjord near modern Stavanger, an event conventionally dated around 872 but placed by many historians somewhat later, perhaps in the 880s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Heimskringla says that Kjotve the Rich, king of Agder, joined the western coalition defeated by <b>Harald Fairhair</b> at Hafrsfjord, traditionally around 872.</blockquote><a href="/state/agder">Agder</a></li><li><blockquote>Håkon Grjotgardsson, whose family came from Hålogaland, allied with <b>Harald Fairhair</b> in the late 9th century and was made earl of the Trøndelag region, founding a powerful line.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-lade">Earldom of Lade</a></li><li><blockquote>The sagas say that <b>Harald Fairhair</b>, after conquering it, gave the region to his ally Rognvald Eysteinsson as earl in the late 9th century, joining North and South Møre under one lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-more">Earldom of Møre</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Orkneyinga saga, <b>Harald Fairhair</b> granted the islands to Earl Rognvald of Møre, who passed them to his brother Sigurd, the first earl, in the late 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Heimskringla, its king Audbjørn fell fighting <b>Harald Fairhair</b> at the Battle of Solskjel, and the district then passed under Harald's earls.</blockquote><a href="/state/firdafylke">Firdafylke</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vestfold. Died in Rogaland. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This girl has not spoken or done so much amiss that she should be punished, but rather she should be thanked for her words.&#8221;</p><footer>Saga of Harald Harfager (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; on the girl Gyda, who would not marry him until he ruled all Norway; it moved him to vow he would not cut his hair until he did, as Snorri Sturluson tells it three centuries later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
